## Why This Question Is Asked
Assesses the ability to design a constraint solver for 3-valued Kleene logic, demonstrating handling of partial knowledge and undefined states in logical policies.

## Key Concepts
- 3-valued truth tables for ¬, ∨, ∧, and →
- Arc-consistency enforcement on ternary domains
- Fixed-point propagation without backtracking

## Implementation
```javascript
const order = { 'F': 0, 'U': 1, 'T': 2 };

function not(v) {
  return v === 'F' ? 'T' : v === 'T' ? 'F' : 'U';
}

function max(a, b) {
  return order[a] > order[b] ? a : b;
}

function implies(a, b) {
  return max(not(a), b);
}

function supports(constraint, varIndex, value, currentAssignment) {
  const vars = ['A', 'B', 'C', 'D'];
  for (const val of ['F', 'U', 'T']) {
    currentAssignment[vars[varIndex]] = val;
    if (constraint(currentAssignment) !== 'F') return true;
  }
  return false;
}

function arcConsistency(domains, constraints) {
  let changed = true;
  while (changed) {
    changed = false;
    for (const constraint of constraints) {
      for (const [varName, domain] of Object.entries(domains)) {
        const filtered = domain.filter(val => 
          supports(constraint, varName, val, { A: domains.A[0], B: domains.B[0], C: domains.C[0], D: domains.D[0] })
        );
        if (filtered.length !== domain.length) {
          domains[varName] = filtered;
          changed = true;
        }
      }
    }
  }
  return domains;
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to reason with Kleene 3-valued logic in a realistic policy-engine style, including short-circuit semantics and propagation.

## Key Concepts
- Kleene 3-valued logic: ¬ flips T/F, U→U; ∧/∨ follow min/max with T>U>F; A→B as ¬A ∨ B.
- Short-circuit evaluation in rule engines.
- Backtracking propagation for constraint satisfaction in a 3-valued setting.

## Code Example
```javascript
function kleeneNot(a){ return a === 'T' ? 'F' : a === 'F' ? 'T' : 'U' }
function kleeneOr(a,b){ /* min/max with T>U>F */ return (a === 'T' || b === 'T') ? 'T' : (a === 'U' || b === 'U') ? 'U' : 'F' }
function kleeneAnd(a,b){ return (a === 'F' || b === 'F') ? 'F' : (a === 'U' || b === 'U') ? 'U' : 'T' }
function implies(a,b){ // ¬a ∨ b
  const na = kleeneNot(a)
  return kleeneOr(na, b)
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How does the answer change if short-circuiting is disabled? 
- How would you extend to detect unsatisfiable sets with more variables?

## Why This Is Asked

Temporal-like constraints on feature flags across release cycles test three-valued propagation and branching heuristics—skills essential for configuration validation at scale.

## Key Concepts

- Kleene three-valued logic with implication defined as ¬A ∨ B
- Propagation rules for ¬, ∨, ∧, and →
- Backtracking search treating U as a first-class domain value

## Code Example

```javascript
// Three-valued backtracking propagator sketch
const VALUES = { T: 2, U: 1, F: 0 };
const NOT = { T: 'F', F: 'T', U: 'U' };
const OR = (a, b) => VALUES[a] >= VALUES[b] ? a : b;
const AND = (a, b) => VALUES[a] <= VALUES[b] ? a : b;
const IMPLIES = (a, b) => OR(NOT[a], b);

function propagate(constraints, assignment) {
  let changed = true;
  while (changed) {
    changed = false;
    for (const [ante, conseq] of constraints) {
      const a = assignment[ante], c = assignment[conseq];
      if (a === 'F' && c !== 'F') { assignment[conseq] = 'F'; changed = true; }
      if (a === 'T' && c !== 'T') { assignment[conseq] = 'T'; changed = true; }
      if (c === 'F' && a !== 'F') { assignment[ante] = 'F'; changed = true; }
    }
  }
  return assignment;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How does the branching heuristic affect worst-case complexity?
- How would you extend this to N time steps?

